    "id": "jlr-scanner-relayed-route-hypotheses",
    "title": "Hypothesis: 2014-and-later buses are reached through the gateway — high-speed via hs-can, medium-speed via ms-can",
    "source_type": "unverified_research",
    "origin": "JLR Scanner reasoning over ISO 15765-4, the SDD platform documents and the MongoosePro JLR pinout (ADR-0015); for the medium-speed buses, a community statement relayed by the owner on 2026-09-05 (docs/research/sdd/COMMUNITY_NOTES.md)",
    "source_locator": "repository:docs/adr/ADR-0015-gateway-relayed-routes-and-live-uds-reads.md",
    "content_fingerprint": null,
    "acquired_on": "2026-09-03",
    "declared_vehicle_programs": [],
    "provenance": "Not a documented fact. ISO 15765-4 puts the legislated diagnostic bus on J1962 pins 6 and 14; SDD places the PCM on PT_HSCAN and declares no gateway routine for PT_HSCAN, CH_HSCAN, HY_HSCAN or CO_HSCAN, addressing their modules directly; the JLR variant of the adapter has two CAN pairs. Hence the hypothesis that modules on these buses are reached through route hs-can, relayed by the vehicle gateway, at the diagnostic CAN's 500 kbit/s. Confirmed by the first read-only response on such a vehicle; refuted by its absence. The medium-speed buses BO_MSCAN and CO_MSCAN were not hypothesised until 2026-09-05, when a community statement placed both behind the gateway on J1962 pins 3 and 11: they are now hypothesised through route ms-can at 125 kbit/s on that statement's strength alone, and stay Unverified until a module on one of them answers.",
    "redistribution_status": "permitted",
    "notes": "Records here resolve as Unverified and the survey shows them as a hypothesis, never as a reachable route."
  },
